<div>
    <p>Proving God exists — and why Descartes needs to</p>
    <img src="/phil1301/assets/images/guido-reni-archangel-michael-defeating-satan-c1636-meisterdrucke-171786.jpg" alt="Guido Reni, Archangel Michael Vanquishing Satan, c. 1636. A radiant, armored archangel stands over a prostrate figure, spear raised, gazing heavenward — an image of divine power and perfection." width="860" height="300" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image">
    <div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
        <p>Read this page alongside or after Meditations III, IV, and V. These are the densest meditations in the text — the <strong>Descartes and Degrees of Reality</strong> handout will be essential for Meditation III. Read it before you begin.</p>
    </div></div>
    <div class="tabs"><div class="tabs__bar" role="tablist"><button class="tabs__tab tabs__tab--active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-0" id="tabs-0-tab-0">▸ 1 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Why Descartes needs God</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-1" id="tabs-0-tab-1">▸ 2 &nbsp;—&nbsp; The causal argument for God (Meditation III)</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-2" id="tabs-0-tab-2">▸ 3 &nbsp;—&nbsp; Error and a second proof of God (Meditations IV–V)</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-3" id="tabs-0-tab-3">▸ 4 &nbsp;—&nbsp; The Cartesian Circle</button><button class="tabs__tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="tabs-0-panel-4" id="tabs-0-tab-4">▸ 5 &nbsp;—&nbsp; What to watch for in Meditation VI</button></div><div class="tabs__panel tabs__panel--active" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-0" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-0"><div>
            <p>At the end of Meditation II, Descartes knows exactly one thing with certainty: he exists as a thinking thing. Everything else — the external world, the reliability of his own reasoning, the validity of mathematics — is still in doubt. The Evil Demon hypothesis has not been defeated. It has only been held at arm’s length by the cogito.</p>
            <p>This creates a serious problem. Descartes’ entire project depends on using reason to rebuild knowledge from the ground up. But if a powerful deceiver might be corrupting his reasoning faculty, then every conclusion he draws — including the ones he draws in Meditations III through VI — might be systematically false. He needs something that rules out the possibility of a deceiving demon before he can trust his own mind.</p>
            <p>That something is God. If Descartes can prove that a perfectly good, all-powerful God exists and is the source of his cognitive faculties, then those faculties cannot be designed to deceive him. A good God would not create a rational creature whose clearest and most careful reasoning was unreliable. God, in other words, is the guarantee that <strong>clear and distinct perception</strong> tracks the truth. Without that guarantee, the whole project of the <em>Meditations</em> collapses.</p>
            <div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>The structure of Meditations III–V</p>
                <p>Meditation III argues for God’s existence from causation. Meditation IV explains how error is possible in a world made by a non-deceiving God. Meditation V offers a second, independent argument for God’s existence from the nature of the concept itself. Together they form the bridge between the isolated certainty of the cogito and the rebuilt world of Meditation VI.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-1" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-1" hidden=""><div>
            <p>Descartes’ first argument for God’s existence begins from a fact he is certain of: he has an <em>idea</em> of God in his mind. This is an idea of a being that is infinite, eternal, omniscient, omnipotent, and supremely good — the most perfect being conceivable.</p>
            <p>Now Descartes applies a causal principle: <strong>a cause must have at least as much reality as its effect.</strong> You cannot get more out of a cause than was put in. A fire heats a room because the fire has more heat than the air; a sculptor produces a statue because the sculptor has more reality (as an existing agent) than the stone arrangement that results. This principle, Descartes thinks, is something he perceives clearly and distinctly, and so he can rely on it.</p>
            <p>Applied to his idea of God: that idea has a specific content — infinite perfection. What could cause an idea of infinite perfection? Not Descartes himself, who is finite and imperfect. Not the external world, which is also finite. Only something with infinite perfection could produce an idea of infinite perfection. Therefore, a being of infinite perfection — God — must exist as the cause of the idea in his mind.</p>
            <div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>Important: degrees of reality</p>
                <p>This argument depends on Descartes’ distinction between <strong>formal reality</strong> (what a thing actually is) and <strong>objective reality</strong> (the content of an idea, considered as a representation). The <strong>Descartes and Degrees of Reality</strong> handout explains these concepts in detail. If the distinction is unclear, the argument in Meditation III will not make sense. Read the handout first.</p>
            </div></div>
            <p>This is a difficult argument, and it has attracted fierce criticism. The causal principle Descartes uses — that a cause must contain as much reality as its effect — is not obviously true. Can you think of a counterexample? A finite being imagining infinite space? A child conceiving of a number larger than any she has ever encountered? Whether those examples defeat the argument is worth thinking through carefully.</p>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-2" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-2" hidden=""><div>
            <h3>Meditation IV: the problem of error</h3>
            <p>Having argued that God exists and is not a deceiver, Descartes faces an immediate objection: if God is not a deceiver, why do humans make errors? We form false beliefs constantly. How is that compatible with a perfectly good creator?</p>
            <p>Descartes’ answer distinguishes between the <strong>intellect</strong> and the <strong>will</strong>. The intellect presents ideas; the will judges them to be true or false. God gave Descartes a finite intellect — one that does not perceive everything clearly and distinctly — but a will that is unlimited in scope. Error occurs when Descartes extends his will beyond his intellect: when he affirms or denies something that he does not yet perceive clearly and distinctly. The fault lies not in God’s design but in the misuse of his own freedom. If he restricts his judgments to what he perceives clearly and distinctly, he will never err.</p>
            <h3>Meditation V: the ontological argument</h3>
            <p>Meditation V offers a second, independent proof of God’s existence — one that does not depend on causal reasoning at all. This is a version of the <strong>ontological argument</strong>, originally formulated by Anselm of Canterbury in the eleventh century.</p>
            <p>The argument runs roughly as follows. God is defined as the most perfect being conceivable. Existence is a perfection — a being that exists is greater than an otherwise identical being that does not. Therefore, if we conceive of the most perfect being, that being must exist: a God who lacked existence would not be the most perfect being conceivable, and would contradict our own definition.</p>
            <p>Descartes frames this in terms of essence and existence. Just as the essence of a triangle includes having interior angles that sum to 180 degrees — you cannot coherently conceive of a triangle without that property — the essence of God includes existence. God’s existence follows from his nature the way mathematical truths follow from definitions.</p>
            <div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>The famous objection</p>
                <p>Immanuel Kant later argued that existence is not a predicate — not a property a thing can have or lack. You cannot define something into existence any more than you can define a hundred imaginary dollars into your bank account. This objection, if successful, cuts the ground from under the ontological argument entirely. Whether Kant is right is one of the enduring debates in philosophy of religion.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-3" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-3" hidden=""><div>
            <p>One of the most serious objections to Descartes’ entire project is known as the <strong>Cartesian Circle</strong>, first pressed by the theologian Antoine Arnauld in the set of objections Descartes published alongside the <em>Meditations</em>. The problem is this:</p>
            <div>
                <p>Descartes uses clear and distinct perception to prove that God exists.</p>
                <p>He then uses God’s existence to guarantee that clear and distinct perception is reliable.</p>
                <p>But if the reliability of clear and distinct perception is in doubt, the proof of God is in doubt. And if the proof of God is in doubt, the guarantee of clear and distinct perception fails.</p>
            </div>
            <p>In other words, the argument seems to go in a circle: reason validates God, God validates reason. Each step depends on the other. This is a textbook case of <strong>circular reasoning</strong> — one of the most fundamental logical errors.</p>
            <div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>Logic in action: circular reasoning</p>
                <p><strong>Circular reasoning</strong> (also called <em>begging the question</em>) occurs when an argument assumes — directly or indirectly — the very conclusion it is trying to prove. The problem is not that the conclusion is false; it may be true. The problem is that the argument provides no independent support for it. You will recall the same fallacy appeared in the <em>Euthyphro</em>, when Euthyphro defined piety as what is pleasing to the gods and then explained why the gods are pleased by piety. See the <strong>Logic Reference</strong> page for the full definition.</p>
            </div></div>
            <p>Descartes scholars have offered various responses to the Circle. One influential reply is that Descartes only needs God to validate <em>remembered</em> clear and distinct perceptions — things he is not currently attending to directly. While he is actively perceiving something clearly and distinctly, he does not need God’s guarantee; the perception is self-validating in the moment. God’s role is to assure him that those past perceptions were also reliable. Whether this reply fully escapes the Circle is debated. It is worth deciding for yourself.</p>
        </div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-4" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-4" hidden=""><div>
            <p>With God established as the guarantor of reason, Descartes is finally in a position to do what Meditation I stripped away: affirm the existence of the external world, and settle the question of what the relationship between mind and body actually is.</p>
            <p>Meditation VI is in many ways the payoff of the entire project. But it is also where the most difficult and unresolved questions surface. The mind/body relationship Descartes defends — substance dualism — raises an immediate problem he cannot fully solve: if mind and body are genuinely distinct substances, how do they interact? How does a thought cause an arm to move?</p>
            <div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">ℹ️</span><div class="callout__content">
                <p>Looking ahead → Meditation VI</p>
                <p>As you read Meditation VI, watch for the <strong>real distinction argument</strong> — the argument that mind and body are genuinely separate substances, not just different aspects of one thing. Then ask: what follows from that? If they are truly separate, what holds them together? This problem — the mind-body problem — is very much alive in philosophy today.</p>
            </div></div>
        </div></div></div>
    
    
    
    
